WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.636
Welcome to Module 6 of the
Provider Notices learning path:

2
00:00:03.636 --> 00:00:06.250
Google incidents and the maintenance gap.

3
00:00:06.250 --> 00:00:09.985
This is Gate 5 of the provider
notices implementation guide,

4
00:00:09.985 --> 00:00:12.525
with Chapter 5 of the developer workbook.

5
00:00:12.525 --> 00:00:15.676
Google Cloud's Personalized
Service Health lists the

6
00:00:15.676 --> 00:00:18.687
incidents relevant to a
project or an organization.

7
00:00:18.687 --> 00:00:19.926
That's all it lists.

8
00:00:19.926 --> 00:00:22.912
So this gate is half
collection and half honesty.

9
00:00:22.912 --> 00:00:26.530
In this module you'll read a
fictional project's incidents,

10
00:00:26.530 --> 00:00:28.941
show that only one kind can ever arrive,

11
00:00:28.941 --> 00:00:33.763
and watch the kit refuse an organization-level
call that nobody has verified.

12
00:00:33.763 --> 00:00:38.201
The gaps get written down with
the ISSO, not papered over.

13
00:00:38.201 --> 00:00:40.535
It takes about forty-five minutes,

14
00:00:40.535 --> 00:00:43.788
with a lab on fixtures and
a lab in a test project.

15
00:00:43.788 --> 00:00:49.690
The code is illustrative: tested against
fixtures, not run against a live project.

16
00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:56.065
Why does Google Cloud need
a module about a gap?

17
00:00:56.065 --> 00:00:57.665
Because of two facts.

18
00:00:57.665 --> 00:01:02.074
First, Personalized Service Health's
event category has one value,

19
00:01:02.074 --> 00:01:05.440
incident, meaning a service
outage or degradation.

20
00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:09.365
Planned maintenance isn't in it,
and neither are security notices.

21
00:01:09.365 --> 00:01:12.599
Those reach a Google Cloud
project by other channels,

22
00:01:12.599 --> 00:01:15.465
such as the contacts set
in Essential Contacts.

23
00:01:15.465 --> 00:01:22.655
Second, Assured Workloads' supported-products
page lists Personalized Service Health

24
00:01:22.655 --> 00:01:28.565
under twenty-five regional packages,
including US Data Boundary, but not

25
00:01:28.565 --> 00:01:33.490
under FedRAMP Moderate, FedRAMP High, I
L 2, I L 4, I L 5, C J I S or I T A R.

26
00:01:33.490 --> 00:01:35.925
A workload in one of those packages has no

27
00:01:35.925 --> 00:01:38.503
Personalized Service
Health to read at all.

28
00:01:38.503 --> 00:01:42.602
Both gaps share a danger: an
empty list looks like good news.

29
00:01:42.602 --> 00:01:45.772
So this gate collects
what Google publishes,

30
00:01:45.772 --> 00:01:49.390
and writes down, with the
ISSO, what can't arrive.

31
00:01:53.140 --> 00:01:56.053
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module.

32
00:01:56.053 --> 00:02:00.852
You'll name the role and the one-time
A P I enablement the reader needs.

33
00:02:00.852 --> 00:02:04.991
You'll collect a project's incidents
and read their products and locations.

34
00:02:04.991 --> 00:02:08.971
You'll explain why Google Cloud's
planned maintenance isn't in this gate,

35
00:02:08.971 --> 00:02:11.265
and where it reaches the project instead.

36
00:02:11.265 --> 00:02:13.880
You'll say which Assured
Workloads packages don't

37
00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:16.877
include Personalized Service
Health, and what to record.

38
00:02:16.877 --> 00:02:19.182
And you'll show that the kit refuses an

39
00:02:19.182 --> 00:02:22.303
unverified call unless
you pass allow unverified.

40
00:02:22.303 --> 00:02:26.132
Before you start, finish Module 1,
on the record and the six kinds,

41
00:02:26.132 --> 00:02:29.741
and read the honest limits
section of the provider notices

42
00:02:29.741 --> 00:02:33.277
chapter in Compliance Automation
and Day-Two Operations.

43
00:02:33.277 --> 00:02:37.440
Lab 1 needs PowerShell 7.1
or later with Pester 5.

44
00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:38.920
No project.

45
00:02:42.670 --> 00:02:44.895
Unit 1 is the guide's Step 5.

46
00:02:44.895 --> 00:02:47.370
The project owner does two things once.

47
00:02:47.370 --> 00:02:50.022
Enable the Service Health
A P I on the project,

48
00:02:50.022 --> 00:02:54.395
and grant the reader's group the
Personalized Service Health Viewer role.

49
00:02:54.395 --> 00:02:55.633
Then two steps.

50
00:02:55.633 --> 00:02:58.963
One: cross-check the list
with Google's own command,

51
00:02:58.963 --> 00:03:03.919
G cloud service health events list, for
the project, in the global location.

52
00:03:03.919 --> 00:03:07.157
Two: collect, with the
provider set to Google.

53
00:03:07.157 --> 00:03:11.832
For a whole organization, set the settings
file's Google scope to organization,

54
00:03:11.832 --> 00:03:14.133
and grant the role on the organization.

55
00:03:14.133 --> 00:03:18.496
Otherwise, grant it at the project:
the viewer role on the organization,

56
00:03:18.496 --> 00:03:22.107
for a project read, is more read
scope than the reader needs.

57
00:03:22.107 --> 00:03:25.331
The gate validates when products
and locations come from

58
00:03:25.331 --> 00:03:28.219
the event's impacts, and
closed incidents are closed.

59
00:03:28.219 --> 00:03:31.520
The gate owner is the
Google Cloud project owner.

60
00:03:35.270 --> 00:03:37.020
Unit 2 is the reader.

61
00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:40.714
Google's Event resource has
the one category, a state,

62
00:03:40.714 --> 00:03:44.408
a detailed category, relevance,
impacts and updates.

63
00:03:44.408 --> 00:03:47.446
Here's what Google leaves to
you, and what the kit adds.

64
00:03:47.446 --> 00:03:51.279
With one category, every
Google notice is an incident,

65
00:03:51.279 --> 00:03:56.420
and the gap is written down in the guide,
the chapter and the ISSO's record.

66
00:03:56.420 --> 00:03:59.526
Impacts come as product
and location objects;

67
00:03:59.526 --> 00:04:02.633
they become the record's
services and regions.

68
00:04:02.633 --> 00:04:05.112
An event's description is sometimes empty,

69
00:04:05.112 --> 00:04:07.945
so the kit takes the latest
update's description.

70
00:04:07.945 --> 00:04:10.832
There's no field that
says action is required,

71
00:04:10.832 --> 00:04:14.890
and the kit adds no action rule
for Google: an open incident that

72
00:04:14.890 --> 00:04:18.870
names resources routes by the
agency's own routing, in Module 7.

73
00:04:18.870 --> 00:04:23.165
Google's A P I returns a response
object and G cloud a bare list,

74
00:04:23.165 --> 00:04:24.883
and the kit accepts both.

75
00:04:24.883 --> 00:04:26.919
A closed state closes the record,

76
00:04:26.919 --> 00:04:31.670
and the detailed category and relevance
are kept as the provider's severity.

77
00:04:31.670 --> 00:04:34.417
The kit has no separate
government endpoint for

78
00:04:34.417 --> 00:04:37.580
Personalized Service
Health; the cloud is global.

79
00:04:41.330 --> 00:04:43.193
Two guards make this gate honest.

80
00:04:43.193 --> 00:04:45.368
First, the organization call.

81
00:04:45.368 --> 00:04:50.168
Google's REST reference documents it, but
the G cloud flags for it weren't read.

82
00:04:50.168 --> 00:04:52.799
So the kit's call list
marks it unverified,

83
00:04:52.799 --> 00:04:57.367
and the live invoker refuses to run
it unless you pass allow unverified.

84
00:04:57.367 --> 00:04:59.805
The kit doesn't run a command on a guess.

85
00:04:59.805 --> 00:05:02.980
That's the guide's interim
path for an organization.

86
00:05:02.980 --> 00:05:04.567
Second, the gaps.

87
00:05:04.567 --> 00:05:07.259
Every project records
that Google's planned

88
00:05:07.259 --> 00:05:09.805
maintenance doesn't come
through this A P I.

89
00:05:09.805 --> 00:05:15.259
And any workload in an Assured Workloads
FedRAMP or I L package records that it has

90
00:05:15.259 --> 00:05:20.793
no Personalized Service Health to read;
the ISSO decides the compensating channel.

91
00:05:20.793 --> 00:05:24.926
The guide lists the danger plainly:
Google maintenance assumed covered,

92
00:05:24.926 --> 00:05:27.681
and a maintenance window
lands with nobody told.

93
00:05:27.681 --> 00:05:30.617
The guard is the gap
itself, in the record,

94
00:05:30.617 --> 00:05:34.355
in the digest's provider
table and in the ISSO's file.

95
00:05:34.355 --> 00:05:40.120
Until the A P I carries maintenance, it
reaches the agency by the contacts it sets.

96
00:05:43.870 --> 00:05:44.595
Try it.

97
00:05:44.595 --> 00:05:48.642
Lab 1 runs in the provider
notices folder of the examples,

98
00:05:48.642 --> 00:05:53.033
in PowerShell 7, for a fictional
project, contoso example prod.

99
00:05:53.033 --> 00:05:55.421
Nothing signs in or sends a request.

100
00:05:55.421 --> 00:05:57.982
Step one lists what Google Cloud needs.

101
00:05:57.982 --> 00:05:58.970
Two calls.

102
00:05:58.970 --> 00:06:03.551
The project call needs the Personalized
Service Health Viewer role on the project,

103
00:06:03.551 --> 00:06:06.207
for the events list
permission, and it's verified.

104
00:06:06.207 --> 00:06:09.965
The organization call needs the
same role on the organization,

105
00:06:09.965 --> 00:06:11.194
and it isn't verified.

106
00:06:11.194 --> 00:06:15.195
Step two collects Google Cloud
from the day-one fixtures:

107
00:06:15.195 --> 00:06:19.195
two notices, one open, none
needing action, both incidents.

108
00:06:19.195 --> 00:06:21.345
Step three reads the records.

109
00:06:21.345 --> 00:06:25.059
Cloud SQL connection errors
in u s east four is open,

110
00:06:25.059 --> 00:06:27.370
with its product and its location.

111
00:06:27.370 --> 00:06:31.120
Cloud Storage latency in
u s central one is closed.

112
00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:36.098
None needs action by Google's word,
because Google has no field that says so;

113
00:06:36.098 --> 00:06:41.360
an open incident that names resources
is routed by the agency's own rules.

114
00:06:45.110 --> 00:06:48.972
Step four collects the second
week, as of November sixteenth.

115
00:06:48.972 --> 00:06:52.011
The same two incidents,
and now both are closed.

116
00:06:52.011 --> 00:06:55.324
The Cloud SQL incident
ended on November ninth,

117
00:06:55.324 --> 00:06:59.822
at thirteen hundred U T C, an hour
after the first week's read saw it open.

118
00:06:59.822 --> 00:07:04.734
That's the honest limit of a weekly
read: it sees an incident after the fact.

119
00:07:04.734 --> 00:07:09.753
The weekly read is for owning and closing
notices, not for detecting incidents,

120
00:07:09.753 --> 00:07:13.073
which still reach the desk
as calls and alerts first.

121
00:07:13.073 --> 00:07:15.684
Step five groups both weeks by kind.

122
00:07:15.684 --> 00:07:18.160
Four records, and every one an incident.

123
00:07:18.160 --> 00:07:20.210
That's not the fixtures being thin.

124
00:07:20.210 --> 00:07:23.723
It's the A P I: its
category has one value.

125
00:07:23.723 --> 00:07:28.358
Maintenance, retirements and security
notices never come through this A P I,

126
00:07:28.358 --> 00:07:33.140
whatever the project's estate looks
like, and that's the gap you write down.

127
00:07:36.890 --> 00:07:40.627
Step six asks the live invoker
for the organization call.

128
00:07:40.627 --> 00:07:45.322
You set the Google scope to organization,
give a fictional organization number,

129
00:07:45.322 --> 00:07:49.803
and build a live invoker, skipping the
sign-in check because nothing will run.

130
00:07:49.803 --> 00:07:52.691
The kit refuses before G cloud starts.

131
00:07:52.691 --> 00:07:56.615
The call isn't verified against
its provider's documentation:

132
00:07:56.615 --> 00:08:01.310
the G cloud group for organization
events is listed on the beta reference,

133
00:08:01.310 --> 00:08:04.928
and its exact flags weren't
read; the REST call is verified.

134
00:08:04.928 --> 00:08:07.190
Pass allow unverified to run it.

135
00:08:07.190 --> 00:08:11.828
That's a decision someone makes on
purpose, in a test project, not a default.

136
00:08:11.828 --> 00:08:15.616
Step seven builds the project
command without running it:

137
00:08:15.616 --> 00:08:20.589
service health events list, for the
project, in the global location, as JSON.

138
00:08:20.589 --> 00:08:22.115
That's the verified call.

139
00:08:22.115 --> 00:08:26.590
Step eight runs this chapter's
tests: eight pass, none fail.

140
00:08:26.590 --> 00:08:28.790
Then delete the output folder.

141
00:08:32.540 --> 00:08:34.890
Lab 2 reads a real test project.

142
00:08:34.890 --> 00:08:38.162
You need a Google Cloud test
project, never production,

143
00:08:38.162 --> 00:08:41.215
its project owner, and a
Google group for the reader.

144
00:08:41.215 --> 00:08:46.353
Enable the A P I, grant the viewer
role, and sign in as the reader.

145
00:08:46.353 --> 00:08:49.616
Then cross-check Google's own
command against the collection.

146
00:08:49.616 --> 00:08:53.577
The project may have no incidents
at all, and that's a valid result.

147
00:08:53.577 --> 00:08:58.452
Then the part that makes this gate
different: record the gaps with the ISSO.

148
00:08:58.452 --> 00:09:03.277
Whether the project is in an Assured
Workloads FedRAMP or I L package, and in

149
00:09:03.277 --> 00:09:08.102
every case, that Google's planned
maintenance doesn't come through this A P I.

150
00:09:08.102 --> 00:09:12.639
To back it out, step four removes
the role binding and deletes the run.

151
00:09:12.639 --> 00:09:17.528
It leaves the A P I enabled, unless
the project owner decides otherwise.

152
00:09:17.528 --> 00:09:19.678
The authors haven't run Lab 2.

153
00:09:19.678 --> 00:09:23.210
The workbook's Chapter 5
has the exact commands.

154
00:09:26.960 --> 00:09:28.347
The federal requirement.

155
00:09:28.347 --> 00:09:34.404
Gate 5 supports NIST special publication
eight hundred fifty-three S A nine, part c,

156
00:09:34.404 --> 00:09:38.858
monitoring external service
providers on an ongoing basis,

157
00:09:38.858 --> 00:09:43.311
and S I five, part a, receiving
security alerts and advisories.

158
00:09:43.311 --> 00:09:47.327
And recording the gaps supports R
A three, part f: updating the risk

159
00:09:47.327 --> 00:09:52.310
assessment when there are significant
changes to the environment of operation.

160
00:09:52.310 --> 00:09:57.722
The evidence is the permission list and
the role binding; the raw Google response,

161
00:09:57.722 --> 00:10:02.972
the notices and the manifest; the refusal
of a call nobody verified; and the gaps,

162
00:10:02.972 --> 00:10:07.010
no maintenance through the A P
I and no Personalized Service

163
00:10:07.010 --> 00:10:11.210
Health in the FedRAMP or I L
packages, written down with the ISSO.

164
00:10:11.210 --> 00:10:14.172
The gate's output is
evidence for these controls.

165
00:10:14.172 --> 00:10:17.094
Your organization still
sets their parameters,

166
00:10:17.094 --> 00:10:20.235
and the assessor decides
whether each is satisfied.

167
00:10:20.235 --> 00:10:24.629
K S I's are FedRAMP twenty X indicators
for cloud service providers;

168
00:10:24.629 --> 00:10:29.780
the agency lines its evidence up with
them, and doesn't report them itself.

169
00:10:33.530 --> 00:10:35.505
Check yourself with two questions.

170
00:10:35.505 --> 00:10:38.592
First: why does Gate 5
cover incidents only?

171
00:10:38.592 --> 00:10:39.817
The answer is b.

172
00:10:39.817 --> 00:10:42.531
The A P I's event category has one value,

173
00:10:42.531 --> 00:10:45.768
incident, and planned
maintenance isn't in it.

174
00:10:45.768 --> 00:10:49.242
It isn't the kit filtering
anything, and it isn't the role.

175
00:10:49.242 --> 00:10:54.317
Second: a workload is in an Assured
Workloads FedRAMP Moderate package.

176
00:10:54.317 --> 00:10:55.792
What does the agency do?

177
00:10:55.792 --> 00:10:56.718
The answer is b.

178
00:10:56.718 --> 00:10:58.493
Record the gap with the ISSO.

179
00:10:58.493 --> 00:11:03.354
Personalized Service Health isn't among
that package's supported products,

180
00:11:03.354 --> 00:11:08.292
so the project has nothing to read, and
relies on the provider's other channels.

181
00:11:08.292 --> 00:11:13.854
Answer c is the trap this module is about:
an empty list doesn't mean no incidents.

182
00:11:13.854 --> 00:11:16.282
The module page has three more questions,

183
00:11:16.282 --> 00:11:19.140
each linked to the
section that answers it.

184
00:11:22.890 --> 00:11:23.715
To sum up.

185
00:11:23.715 --> 00:11:28.815
You read the reader's role, collected two
incidents and then saw both close, showed

186
00:11:28.815 --> 00:11:33.915
that only one kind ever arrives, and watched
the kit refuse a call nobody verified.

187
00:11:33.915 --> 00:11:37.994
The evidence an ISSO keeps for
Gate 5 is the raw Google response,

188
00:11:37.994 --> 00:11:42.779
the notices as JSON and as a spreadsheet
file, and the manifest, all dated;

189
00:11:42.779 --> 00:11:47.800
the role binding; and the written gaps:
no planned maintenance through the A P I,

190
00:11:47.800 --> 00:11:52.977
and any workload in a package that doesn't
include Personalized Service Health.

191
00:11:52.977 --> 00:11:56.052
Before you ship, work through
the chapter's checklist.

192
00:11:56.052 --> 00:11:58.240
That finishes the five providers.

193
00:11:58.240 --> 00:12:01.365
Next is Module 7: routing to owners.

194
00:12:01.365 --> 00:12:06.677
It takes the notices every provider produced,
and gives the ones that need action an

195
00:12:06.677 --> 00:12:11.580
owner, a severity and a due date, as typed
tasks in the service desk's front door.
